    <p>The restaurant industry illustrates the gap between gross and net margin well. A restaurant might have a 65% gross margin on food — the markup from ingredient cost to menu price is substantial. But after paying kitchen staff, servers, rent, utilities, and equipment maintenance, net margins typically compress to 3–9%. High gross margin and thin net margin is the defining characteristic of the hospitality industry.</p>

    <p>Software businesses show the opposite dynamic — high gross margins that translate into meaningful net margins because the cost of delivering another unit of software is nearly zero once it's built.</p>

